# riggers: player (tools/rig/autorig.py, Doom 3's human joints: a player
# character, def/char_<name>.def), human (the same, a monster or another
# character: def/rig_<name>.def, af/monster_<name>.af; extra= bones at most,
# 8 by default, for what the template has no bone for), lying (a body lying
# on its back, rigged from its poses), dead (tools/rig/dead_rig.py: the dead
# bodies, from joints placed by hand in tools/rig/joints/dead.txt) and creature (tools/rig/
# creature_rig.py: its own bones, found from its animations; bones= at most)
# and vehicle (tools/rig/vehicle_rig.py: a still model's wheels joints of their
# own, driven by g_vehicle.c; front= which way it faces, eyes= the driver's view).
# The name is the monster's classname without monster_ (model_<name> in the
# defs); the IQM is the model's file name without m_, or iqm=. The monsters'
# animations play at 10 frames a second (fps=), their idle and their ways of
# getting about looping; RIG_ANIMS_<name> ("<anim> <fps> [loop];...") sets
# them one by one. surface=metal: a robot (Daikatana's no-blood monsters), its
# hits spark; nohitbox=a,b: no hitbox on those joints (the cambot's antenna);
# merge=F: a creature's parts moving alike within F of its size are one bone
# (creature_rig.py's 0.010; less, more bones: the thunderskeet's tail).
# rootout=1: Daikatana's going over the ground out of the
# animations (they play over the origin); rootout=die,cdie: out of those only,
# jpd:z its height too (the cutscene moves the character up and down by it).
# props=sword: the model's prop (its biggest) a model of its own, <iqm>_sword,
# attached at a joint "sword" (the game drops it as a rigid body);
# props=sword:turned turns it half round its length (its edge forward).
# A validated rig doesn't change: each is made in out/rig/stage/<name>/ and
